  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the intersection points of the parabola \\(x = 34y^2\\) with the piecewise linear function \\(f(x)\\). The function \\(f(x)\\) is periodic with a period of 4, and it is defined as follows:\n\\[ f(x) = \\begin{cases} \nx & \\text{if } x \\in [-1, 1) \\\\\n2 - x & \\text{if } x \\in [1, 3) \n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nThe intersection points occur when \\(x = 34y^2\\), and we need to consider the periodicity of the function. The function \\(f(x)\\) repeats every 4 units, so we only need to consider the interval \\([-1, 1)\\) and \\([1, 3)\\) to find all intersection points.\n\n1. For the interval \\([-1, 1)\\):\n\\[ 34y^2 = x \\]\nSince \\(x\\) must be in \\([-1, 1)\\), we have:\n\\[ -1 \\leq 34y^2 \\leq 1 \\]\n\\[ -\\frac{1}{34} \\leq y^2 \\leq \\frac{1}{34} \\]\n\\[ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}} \\leq y \\leq \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}} \\]\n\n2. For the interval \\([1, 3)\\):\n\\[ 34y^2 = x \\]\nSince \\(x\\) must be in \\([1, 3)\\), we have:\n\\[ 1 \\leq 34y^2 \\leq 3 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{1}{34} \\leq y^2 \\leq \\frac{3}{34} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}} \\leq y \\leq \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}} \\]\n\nSince the function \\(f(x)\\) is periodic with a period of 4, we can consider the interval \\([-1, 1)\\) and \\([1, 3)\\) to find all intersection points. The intersection points occur when \\(x = 34y^2\\) and \\(y\\) is in the intervals \\([-1/\\sqrt{34}, 1/\\sqrt{34}]\\) and \\([1/\\sqrt{34}, \\sqrt{3}/\\sqrt{34}]\\).\n\nThe sum of the \\(y\\)-coordinates of these intersection points can be expressed in the form \\(\\frac{a + b\\sqrt{c}}{d}\\). To find this, we need to sum the \\(y\\)-coordinates from the intervals \\([-1/\\sqrt{34}, 1/\\sqrt{34}]\\) and \\([1/\\sqrt{34}, \\sqrt{3}/\\sqrt{34}]\\).\n\nThe sum of the \\(y\\)-coordinates from the interval \\([-1/\\sqrt{34}, 1/\\sqrt{34}]\\) is:\n\\[ 2 \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}\\right) = 2 \\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{34}}\\right) = \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{34}} = \\frac{4\\sqrt{34}}{34} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{34}}{17} \\]\n\nThe sum of the \\(y\\)-coordinates from the interval \\([1/\\sqrt{34}, \\sqrt{3}/\\sqrt{34}]\\) is:\n\\[ 2 \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}\\right) = 2 \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3} + 1}{\\sqrt{34}}\\right) = \\frac{2(\\sqrt{3} + 1)}{\\sqrt{34}} = \\frac{2(\\sqrt{3} + 1)\\sqrt{34}}{34} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3} \\cdot \\sqrt{34} + 2\\sqrt{34}}{34} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{102} + 2\\sqrt{34}}{34} = \\frac{\\sqrt{102} + \\sqrt{34}}{17} \\]\n\nThe total sum of the \\(y\\)-coordinates is:\n\\[ \\frac{2\\sqrt{34}}{17} + \\frac{\\sqrt{102} + \\sqrt{34}}{17} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{34} + \\sqrt{102} + \\sqrt{34}}{17} = \\frac{\\sqrt{102} + 3\\sqrt{34}}{17} \\]\n\nThus, the sum of the \\(y\\)-coordinates of the intersection points is \\(\\frac{a + b\\sqrt{c}}{d}\\) where \\(a = 3\\), \\(b = 1\\), \\(c = 102\\), and \\(d = 17\\). Since \\(a\\), \\(b\\), \\(c\\), and \\(d\\) are all positive integers, \\(a + b + c + d = 3 + 1 + 102 + 17 = 123\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{123}\n\\]",
